==============================================================================
PART 1: proved analytic bound R(x) <= 1/x for x>0, verified numerically
==============================================================================

  Proof: for t>=x>0, t/x >= 1, so e^{-t^2/2} <= (t/x)*e^{-t^2/2}.
  Integrating: int_x^inf e^{-t^2/2} dt <= (1/x) int_x^inf t*e^{-t^2/2} dt
                                        = (1/x) * [-e^{-t^2/2}]_x^inf = e^{-x^2/2}/x.
  Multiplying by e^{x^2/2}: R(x) = e^{x^2/2} int_x^inf e^{-t^2/2}dt <= 1/x.  QED.

         x                   R(x)                    1/x   R(x)<=1/x?
    1.0000        0.6556795424188                    1.0         True
    2.0000       0.42136922928805                    0.5         True
    5.0000       0.19280810471532                    0.2         True
   10.0000      0.099028596471732                    0.1         True
   20.0000      0.049875925981837                   0.05         True
   31.6228       0.03159124822404      0.031622776603368         True
   50.0000      0.019992009580854                   0.02         True
   89.4427      0.011178942868763      0.011180339887498         True
  200.0000     0.0049998750093738                  0.005         True
PASS (checked, x=31.62=sqrt(1000) and x=89.44=sqrt(8000) included --
      exactly the physical-edge x=sqrt(c) values used in Part 3 below).
  R(0) = sqrt(pi/2) = 1.2533141373155002512  (the global max of R on [0,inf),
  since R is monotonically decreasing -- R'=xR-1<=x*(1/x)-1=0 for x>0,
  R'(0)=-1<0 -- so sup_{x>=0} R(x) = R(0) = sqrt(pi/2), finite.)

